    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Clean Code, by Uncle Bob</title>
      <link>https://torres-xyz.github.io/portfolioblog/posts/clean_code/</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 29 Mar 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://torres-xyz.github.io/portfolioblog/posts/clean_code/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;After having finished &lt;a href=&#34;https://torres-xyz.github.io/portfolioblog/posts/the_pragmatic_programmer/&#34; title=&#34;The Pragmatic Programmer&#34;&gt;The Pragmatic Programmer&lt;/a&gt;, I&amp;rsquo;ve started another fan favorite:&#xA;&amp;lsquo;Clean Code - A Handbook of Agile Software Development&amp;rsquo; by Uncle Bob.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;It starts its introduction with this &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.osnews.com/&#34;&gt;osnews.com&lt;/a&gt; comic:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&#34;https://torres-xyz.github.io/portfolioblog/images/wtfm.jpg&#34; alt=&#34;osnews comic about good code vs bad code&#34;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;It follows this with some of the industry-leading experts take on what they think&#xA;clean code means.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;It then concludes the introduction with the advice that this is a book that should be&#xA;read at one&amp;rsquo;s own pace in order to fully understand the concepts it presents. And&#xA;that&amp;rsquo;s exactly what I plan on doing.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>The Pragmatic Programmer</title>
      <link>https://torres-xyz.github.io/portfolioblog/posts/the_pragmatic_programmer/</link>
      <pubDate>Sat, 10 Feb 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://torres-xyz.github.io/portfolioblog/posts/the_pragmatic_programmer/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&#34;https://torres-xyz.github.io/portfolioblog/images/the_pragmatic_programmer.jpg&#34; alt=&#34;The Pragmatic Programmer book&#34;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A book I&amp;rsquo;ve been meaning to read for a long time arrived last month and I&amp;rsquo;ve finally finished it.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;It was really interesting reading about so many concepts that we devs take for granted today - like DRY -&#xA;in the way they were first introduced to the world.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;One thing that stuck with me was the Broken Window Theory:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&amp;ldquo;Don&amp;rsquo;t leave &amp;ldquo;broken windows&amp;rdquo; (bad designs, wrong decisions, or poor code) unrepaired. Fix each one as soon as it is discovered. If there is insufficient time to fix it properly, then board it up. Perhaps you can comment out the offending code, or display a &amp;ldquo;Not Implemented&amp;rdquo; message, or substitute dummy data instead. Take some action to prevent further damage and to show that you&amp;rsquo;re on top of the situation.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
